    pr_ref:  # e944
    description: "THE MYPY RATCHET CANNOT BLOCK A MERGE, AND HAS NOT BEEN ABLE TO. AGENTS.md\nand the Wave-5 notebooks both describe INV-zogud's ratchet as \"BLOCKING,\ninstrument-pinned to mypy 2.1.x\". On the pipeline that actually gates merges\nit is neither.\n\nEVIDENCE, all read from the tree and from a live auto-pr run, not inferred.\n\n1. THE GATING CHECK IS WOODPECKER. auto-pr's own CI poll on PR #204 named\n   exactly one context and merged on it:\n\n     [10s] waiting: ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker\n     CI Passed!  ->  Merging PR #204\n\n2. WOODPECKER RUNS THE RATCHET IN WARNING MODE.\n   .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml:130\n     scripts/check-mypy-ratchet --mode=warning\n   Per the script's own contract, warning mode reports and exits 0. It cannot\n   fail a pipeline.\n\n3. WOODPECKER ALSO USES THE UNPINNED INSTRUMENT.\n   .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml:129   pip install \"mypy~=2.1\"\n   .github/workflows/ci.yml:267     pip install \"mypy~=2.1.0\"\n   ci.yml carries a comment explaining precisely why the narrow pin exists:\n   the baseline's counts are only comparable against the mypy that produced\n   them. Under ~=2.1 a fresh install can resolve 2.2 or 2.3, at which point\n   the ratchet refuses to compare at all -- and in warning mode that refusal\n   is silent too. So Woodpecker carries both halves of the defect.\n\n4. THE STRICT JOB EXISTS BUT IS NOT WHAT GATES.\n   .github/workflows/ci.yml:273     scripts/check-mypy-ratchet --mode=strict\n   and ci-complete lists mypy in its needs with an explicit failure check. All\n   of that is correct and none of it is consulted by the merge path.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, MEASURED. dev at ffbf8a5b93 produced 682 whole-tree strict errors\nagainst a committed shrink-only baseline of 672. Verified as real drift rather\nthan an environment artifact: the ratchet run at the baseline commit\nd229793409 reproduces 672 exactly in the same environment, and the local mypy\nis the 2.1.0 the baseline records. Ten errors accumulated across sixty commits\ntouching 24 source files, and nothing stopped them because nothing could.\nNine of the ten came from one campaign's own PRs. They are fixed in PR #204\nand the ratchet reads 672, delta +0; the baseline was NOT raised, since a\nshrink-only baseline may only ratchet down (L33).\n\nSECOND HOLE, INDEPENDENT OF THE FIRST: NOTHING RUNS MYPY LOCALLY. Not\n.githooks/pre-commit (tracker schema, five axis-coherence gates, test\nmanifest -- zero mypy references), not .githooks/pre-push (protected-branch\nblock only), not scripts/auto-pr, not the pre-commit or pre-work playbooks.\nAn agent following the documented checklist exactly -- identity, tests at\n100%, changelog, sign-off -- never learns it grew the strict surface. The +10\nwas found only because one session chose to pre-flight the ratchet, which was\ndiscretionary rather than procedure.\n\nREMEDY, OWNER-DIRECTED 2026-08-04: make it the same and strict everywhere.\n  a. .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml -> --mode=strict, and pin mypy~=2.1.0 to match\n     ci.yml. Both halves, since \"the same\" covers the instrument.\n  b. scripts/smart-test runs check-mypy-ratchet FIRST, before slicing or\n     pytest. On a strict violation pytest does NOT run, and the output forbids\n     running it until the regression is cleared -- EXPLICITLY INCLUDING a\n     regression the agent did not cause. The \"not my change\" exit is the one\n     that has to be closed: a breach every agent declines to own is exactly\n     how ten errors survived sixty commits.\n  c. Exit-code policy follows the ratchet's own documented contract: exit 1\n     (regression, or instrument mismatch, which means the gate cannot do its\n     job) blocks; exit 2 (infrastructure -- mypy missing, no source roots)\n     warns and continues, because \"an infra failure is not a type regression\".\n  d. AGENTS.md's smart-test section updated to describe the gate, since it\n     currently documents a checklist that omits the only blocking gate.\n\nHONEST LIMIT OF (b): the gate lives in smart-test, which is what the pytest\nalias invokes, so it covers the normal path. A direct \"python -m pytest\"\nbypasses it. The message forbids that explicitly, but text is the enforcement\nthere, not a mechanism."  # e944
    fields: {}  # e944
