  message: "DONE via PR #206 (dev 0603296456). Both holes closed; behavioural evidence below, since this item is entirely about runtime gate behaviour.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED.\n  .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml -- THREE edits, two of them silent alone:\n    * removed \"failure: ignore\" (Woodpeckers continue-on-error). With it set,\n      --mode=strict exits 1 and the step passes anyway, so flipping the mode\n      by itself would have read as a fix and gated exactly nothing.\n    * --mode=warning -> --mode=strict\n    * mypy~=2.1 -> mypy~=2.1.0, matching ci.yml\n  scripts/smart-test runs check-mypy-ratchet --mode=strict FIRST, before\n  slicing and before pytest. Exit 1 (regression OR instrument mismatch) prints\n  the breach and exits without running pytest. Exit 2 (infrastructure) warns\n  and continues, per the ratchets own documented contract that an infra\n  failure is not a type regression.\n  AGENTS.md, the smart-test playbook and the transcript-change hook summary\n  all updated -- all three described a checklist that omitted the only\n  blocking gate.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE 1, the local gate. With .ci/mypy-strict-baseline.json\ndoctored to zeros:\n\n  $ ./scripts/smart-test\n  SMART_EXIT=1\n  banner printed: \"MYPY RATCHET VIOLATED -- pytest was NOT run, and MUST NOT\n  be run.\"\n  grep -cE \"passed|failed\" over the run log  ->  0\n\nZero test-result lines: pytest genuinely never ran, rather than running and\nbeing ignored. Baseline restored afterwards and confirmed clean against git.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE 2, the CI gate, on this items own PR. PR #206 carries the\nstrict blocking step and Woodpecker reads the pipeline definition from the\nbranch, so the change judged itself:\n\n  auto-pr -> \"waiting: ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker\" -> \"CI Passed!\" -> merged\n\nThat also settles the one hypothesis I could not test locally. Three outcomes\nwere possible: pass at 672 (CI env matches the baseline), fail with different\nnumbers (CI env differs, so the baseline is a LOCAL measurement being compared\nagainst a CI count -- an instrument problem the mypy_version pin cannot catch\nbecause it pins the tool, not the installed dependency set), or the job not\nrunning at all. It passed. CI agrees with the baseline at 672, so the drift\nwas let through by warning mode and not by an environment mismatch.\n\nMUTATION-TESTED, because two assertions guard silent failures and a passenger\ntest would be worse than none. Restoring \"failure: ignore\" fails exactly\ntest_mypy_step_does_not_ignore_failure; loosening the pin fails exactly the\npin test. Neither fires on the other.\n\nTWO WRONG DRAFTS OF ONE TEST, recorded in the test docstring rather than\nquietly replaced. \"No invocation uses warning mode\" first grepped raw file\ntext and failed on all three files, because each one DOCUMENTS why warning\nmode is wrong -- the forbidden string appears in the prose explaining the\nprohibition. The second draft dropped comments and then flagged\ncheck-schema-coverage --mode=warning, a DIFFERENT tool that is legitimately\nadvisory; that version would have forbidden correct configuration, which is\nworse than not testing at all. The shipped version takes the invocation as its\nunit, not the file.\n\nHONEST LIMIT, not a loose end I intend to close silently: the gate lives in\nsmart-test, which is what the pytest alias invokes, so it covers the normal\npath. A direct \"python -m pytest\" bypasses it. The banner forbids that\nexplicitly, but text is the enforcement there, not a mechanism. If that turns\nout to matter in practice it needs its own item."  # ee4e
